sp("V04-B-004", "Science and environment",
   "Glacier-fed rivers first swell and then decline as the ice shrinks, so a temporary surplus should not be mistaken for security.",
   "Easy",
   ["When a mountain glacier begins to shrink, the rivers it feeds do not immediately run low.",
    "For years, sometimes decades, they may actually carry more water than before, because the ice is melting faster than snowfall replaces it.",
    "Towns and farms downstream can easily read this abundance as a sign that all is well.",
    "When the ice has retreated beyond some threshold, less of it remains to melt each summer, and the flows begin to fall year after year.",
    "The moment at which flow stops rising and starts declining is sometimes called peak water.",
    "Its timing matters greatly for planning, since a valley that has expanded its irrigation during the years of plenty will face the decline with more demand than it ever had.",
    "Reservoirs, water-sharing agreements and changes in crops all take years to put in place, which is why the warning has to be heeded while the rivers are still full."],
   "But the extra water is borrowed from a store that is not being refilled.",
   [3, 4, 5, 6],
   "'But' contrasts with the comforting reading of 'this abundance as a sign that all is well', and 'the extra water' refers to the increased flow described earlier. The sentence also prepares for the decline that follows ('When the ice has retreated beyond some threshold'). It belongs at gap (1).",
   {4: "At gap (2) the flows have already begun 'to fall year after year', so there is no longer any 'extra water' to speak of.",
    5: "At gap (3) the paragraph has defined peak water, and 'Its timing' in the next sentence must refer to that moment; the inserted sentence would come between them and break the reference.",
    6: "At gap (4) the preceding sentence has already described a valley facing 'the decline', so the warning that the surplus is temporary comes too late, and 'But' has no reassuring claim to contrast with."},
   "Placing the sentence late in the paragraph as a warning, without noticing that 'But' needs a reassuring claim right before it.",
   "V04B-glacier-peak-water", "The contrast marker 'But' and the phrase 'the extra water' point clearly to one position.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 005
sp("V04-B-005", "Technology",
   "Predictive text nudges writers towards common phrasing, a small effect each time that may add up to more uniform language.",
   "Medium",
   ["Predictive text is usually discussed as a convenience, a way of saving a few keystrokes on a small screen.",
    "Its subtler effect is on what gets written at all.",
    "A suggestion appears, it is roughly what the writer meant, and accepting it costs less effort than typing the word that was actually in mind.",
    "Since the system proposes whatever is most common in the text it learned from, each accepted suggestion pulls the sentence slightly towards the average.",
    "No single choice of this kind matters much, but a habit repeated across millions of messages could make written language noticeably more uniform.",
    "None of this means the tools should be abandoned; for people who find typing slow or painful, they can make writing possible rather than merely quicker.",
    "The point is rather that a convenience which shapes expression deserves to be noticed, and perhaps occasionally refused."],
   "A writer about to call a sunset 'bruised' is offered 'beautiful', the commoner word, and accepts it because it is close enough.",
   [4, 5, 6, 7],
   "The missing sentence is an example of a suggestion pulling a sentence 'slightly towards the average', and the next sentence, 'No single choice of this kind matters much', treats it as one instance before widening to 'millions of messages'. It belongs at gap (1).",
   {5: "Gap (2) is tempting because the sentence is about homogenising, but the paragraph has already moved from one choice to 'millions of messages'; a single example placed after that generalisation reverses the movement from instance to pattern, and 'No single choice of this kind' would have no single choice before it.",
    6: "At gap (3) the paragraph has turned to the value of the tools for people who find typing hard, and an example of a lost word would interrupt the move from that concession to 'The point is rather'.",
    7: "At gap (4) the paragraph has reached its conclusion that the convenience 'deserves to be noticed', and an illustration of the basic mechanism would come too late."},
   "Placing the example after the sentence about millions of messages, as if it illustrated the large-scale claim.",
   "V04B-autocomplete-style", "The example must sit between the mechanism and 'No single choice of this kind'; the later gap is partly attractive.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 006
sp("V04-B-006", "Technology",
   "Mature software must preserve accidental behaviour users depend on, a burden worsened because maintainers cannot know which accidents matter.",
   "Difficult",
   ["Every widely used piece of software eventually discovers that its users depend not only on what it was designed to do but on what it happens to do.",
    "A function that returns results in a particular order, though the documentation never promised any order, will sooner or later be relied upon by someone's program.",
    "Fixing such behaviour, or merely changing it for good reasons, then breaks that program, and from the user's side a fix that breaks things is indistinguishable from a bug.",
    "Maintainers of mature systems therefore end up preserving accidents alongside intentions, and the line between the two blurs with each release.",
    "The cost is not only the effort of carrying old behaviour forward.",
    "Each preserved quirk constrains the design of everything built later, so that a system's newest parts are shaped by decisions nobody remembers making.",
    "Some projects try to escape this guesswork by announcing in advance which behaviours are guaranteed and declaring the rest open to change, a policy that is honest but only partly effective.",
    "Users rarely read such declarations, and a change that breaks enough of them tends to be reversed, whatever the policy said."],
   "Worse than either of these costs, maintainers usually cannot tell which accidents anyone relies on until they remove one and the complaints arrive.",
   [3, 4, 6, 8],
   "'Worse than either of these costs' needs two costs already named: the effort of carrying old behaviour forward and the constraint on later design. The uncertainty it describes is 'this guesswork' that the next sentence says some projects try to escape by announcing 'which behaviours are guaranteed'. Only gap (3) has both costs before it and the response immediately after it.",
   {3: "At gap (1) no cost has been named, so 'either of these costs' has no antecedent, and the sentence would interrupt the inference that 'therefore' draws from the broken program.",
    4: "Gap (2) is tempting because the preceding sentence mentions preserved accidents, but no cost has yet been named, so 'either of these costs' has nothing to refer to; the next sentence, 'The cost is not only', introduces costs for the first time, and 'this guesswork' further on would be cut off from the sentence that describes it.",
    8: "At gap (4) the paragraph has already described the response to the uncertainty and its limits, so stating the uncertainty itself comes after its own remedy, and 'this guesswork' would have nothing before it to refer to."},
   "Placing the sentence right after 'preserving accidents alongside intentions' because the sentence is about accidents that users rely on.",
   "V04B-software-backward-compatibility", "Both the second and third gaps follow a mention of preserved accidents; only 'either of these costs', which needs both named costs, and the forward link to 'this guesswork' separate them.",
   cognitive="Analysis", exams=D)

