sp("V04-B-010", "Arts and literature",
   "An unreliable narrator enriches a story because the reader reconstructs what the narrator reports but fails to understand.",
   "Medium",
   ["A narrator who cannot be trusted would seem to be a liability for a story, since the reader depends on the narrator for nearly everything the story contains.",
    "Yet a good many celebrated works of fiction are narrated by people who misunderstand themselves, conceal what they have done or simply fail to notice what is happening around them.",
    "The device works because a narrator reports more than he or she understands.",
    "The reader assembles a picture from these details that the narrator never draws, and the distance between the two pictures becomes the real subject of the book.",
    "Such reading demands a kind of double attention, following the voice while quietly checking it against the evidence it supplies.",
    "It also depends on trust of a different kind: the reader must believe that the author has planted the evidence deliberately, or the gaps would look like carelessness rather than design."],
   "A servant who insists that his employer was a man of honour may describe, in passing and without comment, the guests who were turned away and the letters that went unanswered.",
   [1, 2, 3, 5],
   "The servant is an example of a narrator who 'reports more than he or she understands': he records the turned-away guests without seeing what they mean. The next sentence needs exactly such an example, since 'these details' has no antecedent without it. So the sentence belongs at gap (3).",
   {1: "At gap (1) the opening claim that an untrustworthy narrator is a liability must be answered directly by 'Yet a good many celebrated works of fiction'; an example placed before that answer would illustrate a principle not yet stated.",
    2: "Gap (2) is tempting because the preceding sentence mentions narrators who 'fail to notice', but the principle the servant illustrates is stated only in the next sentence, and 'these details' would then be separated from the details it refers to.",
    5: "At gap (4) the sentence would come between 'Such reading demands a kind of double attention' and 'It also depends', where 'It' must refer to that reading; the example would also arrive after the details have already been used."},
   "Placing the example right after the list of narrators who fail to notice things, before the principle it illustrates.",
   "V04B-unreliable-narrator", "Needs the reader to match the example to the principle and to see that 'these details' requires it immediately before.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

